          "trace_id": "4661237fca45429f948c990bd4c66400",
          "session_id": "full-mt-m-007",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "An underactive thyroid, also known as hypothyroidism, means your thyroid gland is not making enough thyroid hormones (Source: condition-hypothyroidism).\n\nSymptoms of an underactive thyroid often develop slowly and can include fatigue, feeling cold, weight gain, constipation, dry skin, hair thinning, muscle aches, irregular periods, low mood, and slowed thinking. However, these symptoms can be general and are also common in people without thyroid issues (Source: condition-hypothyroidism).\n\nTo diagnose an underactive thyroid, a clinician typically starts by testing your thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels.\n*   **Overt hypothyroidism** is diagnosed when TSH levels are high and free thyroxine levels are below the normal range.\n*   **Subclinical hypothyroidism** is diagnosed when TSH levels are high but free thyroxine levels are still within the normal range (Source: guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring).\nTesting for thyroid peroxidase antibodies can also help determine if an autoimmune condition is causing the hypothyroidism (Source: guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring).\n\nIn the ICD-10 classification system, disorders of the thyroid gland, including hypothyroidism, are found in Chapter 4, which covers endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases. The specific codes for these conditions range from E00 to E07 (Source: coding-icd10-chapter-04-endocrine).\n\nIf you have concerns about your thyroid, it is important to discuss them with your clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
